# Design System page

The app has a Design System page (menu → "Open Design System"). It lists every component from `src/components/` with a live thumbnail, name, short description, and all its states.

The list lives in one file: `src/design-system/catalog.tsx`.

## When you add or change a component

- New component in `src/components/` → add an entry to `catalog` in the same change. No exceptions.
- New state or variant on an existing component → add a state to its entry.
- Renamed or deleted component → rename or remove its entry.

## Entry shape

```tsx
{
  id: 'chip',                 // url-safe, unique
  name: 'Chip',               // same as the component name
  group: 'Buttons',           // one of `groups`; add a new group only if none fits
  description: 'Small pill that marks a choice.', // one plain sentence
  keywords: ['tag', 'pill'],  // words people might search for
  states: [
    { label: 'Default', render: () => <Chip label="5GB" onPress={() => showToast('Pressed')} /> },
    { label: 'Selected', render: () => <ChipDemo initialSelected /> },
    { label: 'Disabled', render: () => <Chip label="5GB" disabled /> },
  ],
}
```

- The first state is also the list thumbnail. Make it the most typical look.
- States must be the real component, never a drawing of it. They must be tappable and react like in the app. Use a small `...Demo` wrapper with `useState` when a state needs memory.
- Set `backdrop: true` for glass parts so the glass is visible.
- Set `edgeToEdge: true` for parts that already add the screen side padding.
- Use theme tokens, not raw hex or font sizes.

Run `npx tsc --noEmit` and `npx expo lint` after editing the catalog.
